# A cheminformatics approach to characterize metabolomes in stable-isotope-labeled organisms

## abstract
We report a computational approach (implemented in MS-DIAL 3.0; http://prime.psc.riken.jp/) for metabolite structure characterization using fully 13C-labeled and non-labeled plants and LC-MS/MS. Our approach facilitates carbon number determination and metabolite classification for unknown molecules. Applying our method to 31 tissues from 12 plant species, we assigned 1,092 structures and 344 formulae to 3,604 carbon-determined metabolite ions, 69 of which were found to represent structures currently not listed in metabolome databases.

Hiroshi Tsugawa $\textcircled{1}1,2,7\star$ , Ryo Nakabayashi1,7, Tetsuya Mori1, Yutaka Yamada1, Mikiko Takahashi1, Amit Rai $\textcircled{10}3$ , Ryosuke Sugiyama1, Hiroyuki Yamamoto4, Taiki Nakaya3, Mami Yamazaki3, Rik Kooke5, Johanna A. Bac-Molenaar5, Nihal Oztolan-Erol5, Joost J. B. Keurentjes $\Cup$ 5, Masanori Arita1,6 and Kazuki Saito $\textcircled{10}1,3\star$
We report a computational approach (implemented in MS-DIAL 3.0; http:/ prime.psc.riken.jp/) for metabolite structure characterization using fully $13C$ -labeled and nonlabeled plants and LC–MS/MS. Our approach facilitates carbon number determination and metabolite classification for unknown molecules. Applying our method to 31 tissues from 12 plant species, we assigned 1,092 structures and 344 formulae to 3,604 carbon-determined metabolite ions, 69 of which were found to represent structures currently not listed in metabolome databases.
The structure characterization of metabolite ion features requires the untangling of the mass fragmentation of small molecules1. Although plants are rich resources for biotechnology and drug discovery2, the current coverage $(<5\%)$ of mass spectrometry (MS)- based metabolomics is insignificant compared with the chemical space of natural products1,3. Therefore, advances in metabolomics technologies are essential for high-throughput screening of natural products and for a better understanding of plant physiology from metabolite sets in phytochemical genomics.
Liquid chromatography–tandem mass spectrometry (LC–MS/ MS) is the gold standard in metabolomics; the promulgation of MS/ MS records for metabolites has enhanced annotation in biological samples4, but data mining for unknown ions remains an unresolved issue. Complicating matters, $90\%$ of mass signals are regarded as artifacts or redundant ions such as different adduct forms and in-source fragment ions resulting from metabolites5. Moreover, metabolite structure characterization also remains challenging1. One method to increase confidence in annotation is to use fully $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ -labeled and non-labeled living organisms, where true metabolites exhibit a precursor $m/z$ shift consistent with the carbon content of molecules between labeled and non-labeled LC–MS data6. However, few software tools are available7 to tackle the annotation of metabolite ‘structures’ beyond molecular formula predictions.
$N$ -fructosyl peptides in A. cepa. Our results indicate that the FSEA metabolite classification can be effectively used in metabolome network analysis.
Metabolite structure identification is essential for biological interpretation. While comprehensive lipidome analysis has become a routine process by clearly decoding the mass fragmentation of lipids17, the profiling of natural products remains immature as a result of chemical diversity; this is also true for exposome research18. Our study improves the coverage of metabolite characterization beyond the differential analysis of ion features and makes it possible to deepen our understanding of mass fragmentations for natural products. Moreover, the set of a fully labeled plant data repository and a curated mass spectral database will be of value in developing an automated metabolomics workflow using false discovery rate estimations19. Our workflow has the potential to facilitate the screening of metabolomes and the discovery of novel natural products in combination with NMR characterization20 and synthetic chemistry.
