## Methods
Chemical synthesis of $N$ -fructosyl S-(2-carboxypropyl) glutathione. The pH of a stirred solution of glutathione (Wako, 307 mg, $1.0\mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ) in $\mathrm{~H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{^{10ml}},$ ) was adjusted to 9.4 by $4\mathrm{M}\mathrm{NaOH}$ . Methyl 3-bromo-2-methylpropionate (SigmaAldrich, $163\mathrm{mg}$ , ${900}\upmu\mathrm{mol}\dot{}$ ) dissolved in ethanol (Wako, $3\mathrm{ml}$ ) was added dropwise to $3\mathrm{ml}$ of the glutathione solution $(300\upmu\mathrm{mol}),$ , and the clear, colorless solution was stirred at room temperature under nitrogen for $18\mathrm{h}$ (ref. 26). The reaction mixture was acidified to $\mathrm{pH}5–6$ with $10\%$ acetic acid (Wako, ${300\upmu\mathrm{l}};$ and then stored at $-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without further purification. A 1-ml aliquot of this solution containing $47.6\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ was evaporated in vacuo to obtain a colorless oil; it was then dissolved in acetic acid $(500\upmu\mathrm{l})$ , and $\mathbf{D}$ -glucose (Wako, $90.0\mathrm{mg}$ , $500\upmu\mathrm{mol}\$ ) was added. The suspension was vigorously stirred at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ (ref. 27). The reaction mixture was cooled to room temperature, filtered, and concentrated in vacuo to yield a paleyellow oil. After the residue was dissolved in $\mathrm{~H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{iml})$ , $\mathrm{1ml}$ of $4\mathrm{MNaOH}$ was added dropwise; this was followed by $30\mathrm{min}$ of incubation at room temperature. The reaction was quenched by $10\%$ acetic acid $(2\mathrm{ml})$ . The resulting solution was subjected to reversed-phase HPLC (Imtakt Cadenza CD-C18, $2\%$ $\%\mathrm{\DeltaMeCN/H_{2}O}$ with $0.1\%$ formic acid) to yield $N$ -fructosyl S-(2-carboxypropyl) glutathione $\mathrm{3.0mg,}$ $11\%$ over three steps).
LC–MS/MS condition. Samples $\left(3\mathrm{mg}\right)$ of dried plant tissues were weighed in a $2.0\mathrm{-ml}$ microtube (Sarstedt). Their metabolites were extracted with $150\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $80\%$ MeOH containing $2.5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ lidocaine and 10-camphour sulfonic acid using a mixer mill and zirconia beads (7 min at $18\mathrm{Hz}$ and $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (MM300; Retsch)). After $10\mathrm{min}$ of centrifugation at $^{17,800g,}$ the supernatant was filtered using an HLB $\upmu$ Elution plate (Waters) and $100\upmu\mathrm{l}$ extracts were transferred to a glass amber vial featuring a micro-insert (Agilent Technologies) for LC–MS/MS analysis. The extracts $(1\upmu\mathrm{l})$ were analyzed using LC–QTOF/MS (LC, Waters Acquity UPLC System; MS, Waters Xevo G2 Q-Tof). The analytical conditions were LC: column, Acquity bridged ethyl hybrid C18 $(1.7\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , $2.1\mathrm{mm}\times100\mathrm{mm}$ , Waters); solvent system: solvent A (water including $0.1\%$ formic acid) and solvent B (acetonitrile including $0.1\%$ formic acid); gradient program: $99.5\%$ solvent $\mathrm{A}/0.5\%$ solvent B at $0\mathrm{min}$ , $99.5\%\mathrm{A}/0.5\%\mathrm{B}$ at $0.1\mathrm{min}$ , $20\%\mathrm{A}/80\%\mathrm{B}$ at $10\mathrm{min}$ , $0.5\%\mathrm{A}/99.5\%\mathrm{B}$ at $10.1\mathrm{min}$ , $0.5\%\mathrm{A}/99.5\%\mathrm{B}$ at $12.0\mathrm{min}$ , $99.5\%\mathrm{A}/0.5\%\mathrm{B}$ at $12.1\mathrm{min}$ and $99.5\%\mathrm{A}/0.5\%\mathrm{B}$ at $15.0\mathrm{min}$ ; flow rate: $0.3\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at 0 min, $0.3\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{min}$ , $0.4\mathrm{{ml}\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ at $10.1\mathrm{min}$ , $0.4\mathrm{{ml}\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ at $14.4\mathrm{{min}}$ , and $0.3\mathrm{ml}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at $14.5\mathrm{min}$ ; column temperature: $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; MS detection: polarity, positive and negative (two separate measurements); capillary voltage, $+3.00\mathrm{kV}$ (positive) $/{-2.75}\mathrm{kV}$ (negative); cone voltage: $25.0\mathrm{V};$ source temperature: $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ desolvation temperature: $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ cone gas flow: $50\mathrm{lh}^{-1}$ ; desolvation gas flow: $800\mathrm{{lh^{-1}}}$ ; collision energy: $6\mathrm{V}$ ; mass range: $m/z50-1,500\$ ; scan duration: 0.1 s; inter-scan delay: 0.014 s; data acquisition: centroid mode; Lockspray (Leucine enkephalin); scan duration: $1.0s;$ inter-scan delay: 0.1 s.
MS-DIAL 3.0 development. MS-DIAL28 is being developed with feedback from users; feature detection, spectral matching, and peak alignment are continuously improved. New functions were added for this study to (1) reduce the number of false positive peaks, (2) determine the adduct type of metabolites by different polarity MS data, (3) track the isotope-labeled metabolites, (4) examine the precursor ions with characteristic product ions or neutral losses, and (5) execute integrated molecular networking, which utilizes the metabolite ontology, formula oriented bioreaction, and mass spectral similarity. The basic concepts are summarized in earlier reviews1,7,29. We applied them in one package with a userfriendly GUI; it was released as MS-DIAL 3.0. All source codes are in ${\mathrm{C}}\#$ language; technical details are presented below.
Peak character estimator (MS-DIAL-PCE) algorithm. While feature detection is mostly as described elsewhere28, a large amount of empirical source code was written to correctly identify peak left and right edges. After feature detection, the ion charge state is first estimated on specific mass ladders—for example, a 0.5-Da step is applied for double-charged ions and a 0.333-Da step is applied for triple-charged ions. Estimation is performed in the M and $\mathbf{\deltaM}+1$ Da range; M represents the $m/z$ of the precursor ion. For deisotoping (isotopic ion recognition), monotonically decreasing ions from a precursor ion are recognized as isotopic ions until $800\mathrm{Da}$ ; this methodology is not suitable for considering bromide- and chloride-containing metabolites, and therefore the restriction of ‘monotonically decreasing’ can be turned off as an option in MS-DIAL. For the other precursor ions, the isotopic patterns are compared with the theoretical isotopic patterns simulated by the alkane $(\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{H}_{2n+1})$ formula because the monoisotopic ion is not always monitored as the highest intensity. The isotopic ratio $(I_{\mathrm{ratio}})$ is defined as the ratio of $I_{(\mathrm{M}+n)}/I_{(\mathrm{M}+n-1)}.$ and the difference is defined as $\lvert I_{\mathrm{ratio,experiment}}-I_{\mathrm{ratio,theoretical}}$ , where I, $n,I_{\mathrm{ratio,experiment}}.$ and $I_{\mathrm{ratio,theoretical}}$ stand for ion abundance, isotope number, experimental isotopic ratio, and theoretical isotopic ratio, respectively. In this study, the tolerance of the isotopic ratio and the $m/z$ accuracy were set to 5.0 and 0.025, respectively. Diagnostics are executed for $\pm2$ scan points or a $\pm0.0275$ -min region from the focused monoisotopic ion. Survey is performed from the minimum $m/z$ of the MS1 spectra. While multiple charged states $(+8,+9,+10$ , or more) considered in small RNA and proteome studies can be handled by the current MS-DIAL program, 2 was set as the maximum charge state in our study.
Adduct ion recognition is executed by two computational approaches with the user-defined dictionary. For positive ion data we used $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ , $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{Na}]^{+}$ , $[\mathrm{M-H_{2}O+H}]^{+}$ , $[\mathrm{M-C_{6}H_{10}O_{5}+H}]^{+}$ , and $[2\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ ; for negative ion data we used $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ , $[\mathrm{M-H_{2}O-H}]^{-}$ , $[\boldsymbol{\mathrm{M}}+\boldsymbol{\mathrm{HCOOH-H}}]^{-}$ , $[\mathrm{M-C_{6}H_{10}O_{5}-H}]^{-}$ , and $[2\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ . More adduct types can be used in MS-DIAL if needed.
Our first approach applies adduct type information on annotated ions by spectral libraries where the adduct type has already been defined. Mass shifts between the assigned adduct type and user-defined adduct types are searched for in MS1 spectra. Then adduct pairing is performed for the remaining ions where the detection of $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ or $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ is not necessary. All mass shift combinations of adduct pairs—for example, a difference of 180.0025 Da from $(\mathrm{[M-H_{2}O+H]^{+}}$ , $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{10}\mathrm{O}_{5}+\mathrm{H}]^{+})$ , and of $46.0023\mathrm{Da}$ for $([\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-},$ $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{HCOOH-H}]^{-}).$ —are searched for the detected ion features in which the shift from $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ or $[\mathrm{M-H}]\cdot$ − is prioritized. For the remaining unassigned ions, the adduct type is defined as $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ or $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ .
In-source fragment ions and other unexpected adduct ions were grasped on the basis of (1) their chromatogram correlation, (2) the MS1 and MS/MS partial similarity, and (3) the ion abundance correlation among samples. The cosine correlation for chromatogram data points from the peak left to the right edges was calculated when the retention time of the peak tops was within $\pm0.02\mathrm{min}$ . ‘Similar peak shape’ was assigned when the cosine value exceeded 0.95. The MS1 and MS/MS partial similarity was calculated if the MS/MS spectrum was assigned. The similarity score, which integrates the dot product, the reverse dot product, and fragment existence, was calculated between Max (lowest $m/z$ value of MS1, lowest $m/z$ value of MS/MS) and the precursor $m/z$ value where $\operatorname{Max}(\mathbf{A},\mathbf{B})$ indicates the use of the larger value between A and B. Matched MS1 peaks with MS/MS peaks were assigned the term ‘included in the MS/MS spectrum of higher $m/z$ ion’ when the partial similarity was greater than 0.9. Furthermore, the correlation of ion abundances of two alignment spots was calculated for all precursor ion pairs found in $\pm0.02\mathrm{min}$ . The spots were assigned the term ‘highly correlated ions’ when the correlation coefficient was greater than 0.9. In this manner, MS-DIAL associates the peak characteristics with other peak features including ‘similar peak shape’, ‘included in the MS/MS spectrum of higher $m/z$ ion’, and ‘highly correlated ions’. The recommended results are curated with the GUI of MS-DIAL and the molecular networking approach4. In fact, a total of 812 and 254 ion features where the carbon shift was confirmed have already been annotated as in-source fragment ions, while a total of 3,584 and 2,355 peaks were originally registered as the tentative true positives in positive and negative ion mode, respectively. Although we submitted 3,604 carbon-determined metabolite ions as true positive metabolites, there is a possibility that artifacts remain. Therefore, the information is updated in our PlaSMA website as soon as further curation is completed.
Adduct type determination by using different polarity peak features (MS-DIAL-IFA). The determination of the adduct type is a critical issue for unknown metabolite identification. Although the methodology described above is useful for identifying the adduct type, we here created a function to integrate the ion features of positive and negative ion mode: the function was termed the ion feature amalgamator (MS-DIAL-IFA). For example, the list of the retention times and $m/z$ for monoisotopic ions detected in negative ion mode can be imported to the positive ion mode data in MS-DIAL. In addition, users can define the set of positive and negative adduct types: the sets $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}/$ $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ $2.015\mathrm{Da}$ difference), $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}/[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{HCOOH}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ $(-43.99\mathrm{{Da}}$ difference), $[\mathrm{M-H_{2}O+H}]^{+}/[\mathrm{M-H}]^{-}$ − $_{(-15.99\mathrm{Da}}$ difference), $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}/\$ $[\boldsymbol{\mathrm{M}}+\boldsymbol{\mathrm{HCOOH-H}}]^{-}$ $_{-62.00\mathrm{{Da}}}$ difference), $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{NH_{4}}]^{+}/[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ $\mathrm{19.04Da}$ difference), $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{NH_{4}}]^{+}/[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{HCOOH}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}-}26.97\mathrm{D}$ a difference), and $[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{C_{6}}\mathrm{H_{10}}\mathrm{O_{5}}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}/[\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{H}]^{-}(-160.\$ .04 Da difference) were used in this study. Mass differences are detected with the user-defined retention time and $m/z$ tolerances where we used $0.025\mathrm{{min}}$ and $0.01\mathrm{Da}$ , respectively. The function is especially useful when the type is not determined by the adduct-pairing method.
Isotope grouping (MS-DIAL-ISO). The function is designed to group the same metabolite ions from partially labeled, fully labeled, and time course labeling experiments using carbon ${}^{(13}\mathrm{C)}$ , deuterium $(^{2}\mathrm{H})$ , nitrogen $\mathrm{(^{15}N)}$ , oxygen $\mathrm{(^{18}O)}$ , sulfur $\mathrm{(^{34}S)}$ , or combinations thereof. Fully $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ -labeled and non-labeled plant tissues were analyzed in this study. The isotope tracking function was used to determine the exact carbon number of detected metabolites by checking the mass shift.
MS-DIAL peak alignment is first executed where the retention time and $m/z$ tolerances were set to $0.01\mathrm{min}$ and $10{\mathrm{p.p.m}}$ ., respectively. Note that the deisotoping function is not executed during application of the tracking function, and that the identification result for non-labeled data is reflected in the alignment results. In principle, the ideal pattern of integrated isotopologues from completely labeled and non-labeled data is a concave circle mimicking a smile.
However, practically, disturbances from label impurity, ion noises especially in low-abundance peaks, and natural isotopologues of $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ , $^{18}\mathrm{O},$ and $^{34}\mathrm{S}$ should be dealt with computationally. Currently, the aligned spot where the ion abundance is four times higher in non-labeled than completely labeled data is recognized as a monoisotopic ion. The maximum count of the molecular formula element is estimated as follows: the maximum carbon count $(\operatorname{Max}\mathbf{C})$ is defined by (monoisotopic mass/12); maximum hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, and sulfur are defined by (Max $\mathrm{C}\times3.3\mathrm{)}$ , (Max $\mathrm{C}\times1.2\$ , (Max $\mathbf{C}\times2.2\$ , and $(\operatorname{Max}\mathbf{C}\times1.0)$ , respectively. The coefficients are derived from the modified seven golden rules that cover $99.7\%$ of the metabolome molecules described in our earlier report9. Then, the aligned spots where the difference in the retention time and $m/z$ from the monoisotopic ion is less than $0.01\mathrm{min}$ and $10\mathrm{p.p.m}$ . $(1.00335\times n+$ monoisotopic $m/z)$ are recognized as isotopologue candidates; here, $n$ stands for the carbon number from the monoisotopic ion. Finally, the last spot, that is, the fully labeled metabolite peak, is determined on the basis of the following criteria: (1) the $m/z$ value should be greater than the largest $m/z$ value of the isotopic ions of nonlabeled data, (2) searching is finished when there is no candidate spot in the $\mathbf{M}+2$ area where M stands for the temporal last spot in completely labeled data, and (3) the highest $m/z$ peak (M) is excluded if the ion abundance of the $\mathbf{M}-1$ peak is five times greater than that of the M peak; these diagnostics are continued until the $\mathrm{M}-2$ area.
We evaluated the accuracy of adduct type and carbon number determinations by using the level 2.1 and 2.2 annotation results from this study, in which our program was evaluated without bias. A total of 1,260 metabolite ions were used as the correct set; this yielded $91.5\%$ and $92.0\%$ accuracy for identifying adduct types and carbon numbers, respectively. This result indicated that the diagnostics do not always provide the correct cluster of isotopologues; accuracy may be improved by the application of other criteria such as chromatogram correlations. In fact, although we attempted to implement the chromatogram correlation technique, there were no significant improvements. Therefore, we created a user-friendly GUI to curate our results of automatic isotope clustering. We manually checked and appropriately modified the isotope tracking results of all plant tissues, especially for highly co-eluted metabolites or for tricky (noisy) ions.
Searching characteristic product ions and neutral losses (MS-DIAL fragment searcher). To grasp the same metabolite classes defined by their characteristic product ions or neutral losses, we developed a function to find precursor ions containing the product ions and/or neutral losses. For example, if $m/z287.055$ (stands for flavone $^{+40}$ ) ‘AND’ neutral loss 162.0257 Da (stands for hexose) are queried in electrospray ionization (ESI) $(+)$ –MS/MS datasets, most of the extracted metabolite ions would be classified to (flavone $+4\mathrm{O}),$ -glycosides in plant species. The MS-DIAL GUI is also optimized to easily curate the metabolite classes with unique product ions and neutral losses.
The purpose of using MS/MS spectral databases was twofold. First, the integrated MS/MS spectral database was used for metabolite identification/ annotation on the basis of MS/MS similarity matching. Because triple-quadrupole and ion-trap instruments in the databases yield low-accuracy spectra, the MS/MS mass tolerance for metabolite annotations was set to $0.25\mathrm{Da}$ in all queries. Second, the high-quality MS/MS spectra were used for the curation of fragment ions that are used for (1) new fragment-oriented molecular fingerprints (mass fragment fingerprint (MFF)), (2) finding the unique product ions and neutral losses in the metabolite classes, and (3) FSEA to recommend metabolite classes for unknown spectra. Importantly, the metabolite class used in FSEA was manually assigned after human curation, taking into account the annotation (resolution) level of MS-based metabolomics.
MS-FINDER development. MS-FINDER9 is upgraded in this study for the comprehensive annotation of plant-specific metabolites. The upgrades include expanded metabolome structure coverage, the creation of a fragment database, the development of novel molecular fingerprints for structure elucidation, and of FSEA for metabolite class recommendations. The program was released as MS-FINDER 3.0, linked with MS-DIAL and integrated into our computational workflow. Importantly, we did not directly apply the MS-FINDER results. Rather, the confirmation of metabolite structures was performed manually with the interpretation of mass fragmentations. The workflow we present effectively assists in annotation with understanding of the carbon element number, the annotated fragments, the recommended metabolite classes, the suggested structure candidates, the relationship in molecular networking, and the knowledge for plant biosynthetic pathways. When we were unable to assign a structure level annotation despite the detection of several characteristic ions, we assigned level 3.1 (formula determined) after excluding the potential presence of by-products such as insource fragment ions. All of the source code was written in $C\#$ language; details are presented below.
Metabolome structure databases. The curation of metabolome databases is an essential part of metabolite annotation because most unknowns should have already been reported. Owing to advances in database curation for metabolomes, the accuracy of metabolite annotation can be improved by refinement of the structure elucidation algorithm. The current MS-FINDER program (with the count of structure records in parentheses) encompasses KNApSAcK (45,852), UNPD (166,995), PlantCyc (3,817), FooDB (21,943), NANPDB (3,882), STOFF-IDENT (10,231), LipidMAPS (34,466), ChEBI (53,746), PubChem biosynthetic pathway (8,285), DrugBank (6,273), SMPDB (1,490), YMDB (1,840), ECMDB (1,342), T3DB (2,499), BMDB (7,232), and the HMDB family including HMDB (95,924), Urine (3,929), Saliva (1,129), Fecal (1,073), CSF (360), and Serum (23,138). Structure curation was as in our earlier report9. The structures of the HMDB 4.0 family (HMDB, Urine, Saliva, Fecal, CSF, Serum), ChEBI, FooDB, LipidMAPS, PlantCyc, STOFF-IDENT, and YMDB were newly downloaded on 28 January 2018. The structures of BMDB, DrugBank, ECMDB, KNApSAcK, NANPDB, PubChem biosystems and pathways, SMPDB, T3DB, UNPD, and MINE were the same as in the earlier reports9,35. The structure identifiers, including InChI code, SMILES, and SDF formatted in each database, were converted to canonical SMILES codes by ChemAxon molconvert. They were standardized with the ChemAxon standardizer by salt stripping, the removal of fragments and explicit hydrogens, neutralizing, and removing absolute stereo configurations. Then, the standardized SMILES codes were converted to an SDF file. Exact mass, formula, and InChIKey were generated from the SDF file by ChemAxon calculator. Structure records with the same first layer (14 characters) of InChIKey were merged. Namely, stereoisomers including tautomers were not distinguished in the current project: one stereoisomer was registered as the representative structure where the selection depended on the alphabet orders of the second and third layers of InChIKey. Only organic compounds composed of C (must), H, N, O, P, S, N, F, Cl, Br, I, and Si within the range from $m/z50$ to $m/z$ 2,000 were used. In addition, their metabolite ontology was generated for all structures by ClassyFire; the ‘direct parent’ term was used as the metabolite ontology. Finally, 321,616 metabolome structures were entered into a customized database in MS-FINDER; 2,334 ontologies were included. An enzymatically expanded in silico structure database, MINE (count of structure records, 643,307) was also used; it contained 2,057 ontologies.
Fragment database querying $m/z$ and neutral loss with fragment substructures and ontologies. Unique product ions and neutral losses for a certain metabolite class have been used for metabolite annotations. For instance, (1) $m/z78.9585$ and $m/z96.9595$ are used for searching phosphate $\left(\mathrm{PO}_{3}^{-}\right)$ - and sulfate $\mathrm{(HSO_{4}^{-})}$ 1 -containing metabolites, respectively; (2) neutral losses of 146.0579 $\mathrm{(C_{6}H_{10}O_{4})}$ , 162.05282 $\mathrm{(C_{6}H_{10}O_{5})}$ , and 176.0321 $\mathrm{(C_{6}H_{8}O_{6})}$ are used for searching metabolites that contain deoxyhexose (dHex), hexose (Hex), and uronic acid (HexA) moieties; (3) aglycones of the flavonol class, such as kaempferol, quercetin, and isorhamnetin, can be searched by their specific $m/z$ values; for example, 285.0405 $\left(\mathrm{C}_{15}\mathrm{H}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{6}^{-}\right)$ , 301.0354 $\mathrm{(C_{15}H_{9}O_{7}^{-\cdot}}$ and 315.051 $\left(\mathrm{C_{16}H_{11}O_{7}^{-}}\right)$ ) $\left(\mathrm{C_{16}H_{11}O_{7}}^{-}\right)$ . Searching the characteristic product ion and neutral loss values is a popular technique for putative structure characterization in natural product chemistry34 and lipidomics17 research. Consequently, a database for finding specific product ions and neutral losses is needed for wide coverage of various metabolites.
The FSEA algorithm provides information on metabolite class candidates without any structure databases, that is, a complete de novo recommendation. On the other hand, an MS/MS spectrum can belong to several metabolite classes. For example, we found that the MS/MS spectrum of $N$ -fructosyl peptides belongs to not only ‘peptides’ but also ‘hexosyl metabolites’ and ‘amino acid derivatives’. All of the recommended classes can be used totally with the characteristic ions, and therefore, the top 3–5 candidates would partially become the correct classes in the current FSEA program.
The experimental MS/MS spectrum is annotated via the fragment database containing the $m/z$ or neutral loss queries; each query contains the fragment ontology candidates. The assigned ontologies are termed ‘experimental spectrum fingerprint (ESF)’. Here, MSF ∩ ESF becomes true when one of the fragment ontologies listed in an assigned formula is found in MSF. On the other hand, MSF ∪ ESF is calculated from the assigned fragment ontologies to MSF and ESF. The Jaccard score, MSF ∩ ESF/MSF ∪ ESF, is used as the diagnostic criterion for the ranking of molecular structures.
The accuracy of MS-FINDER 3.0 functions was evaluated using the set of publicly available high-quality-MS/MS records containing 4,430 and 1,741 spectra in positive and negative ion mode, respectively. All of the correct structures are included in our customized metabolome structure database (321,616 structures). The MS1 mass tolerance, MS/MS mass tolerance, relative abundance cutoff, and isotopic ratio tolerance were set to $0.01\mathrm{Da}$ , 0.025 Da, $1\%$ , and $20\%$ , respectively. Filtering of ‘LEWIS and SENIOR check’ and ‘common range for element ratio check’ was activated. The targeted atoms were set as C, H, O, N, S, and P. Tree depth was set to 2. For the batch process, the top five formula candidates were transferred to the structure searching procedure. The customized metabolome databases, including the HMDB family (containing Urine, Saliva, Feces, Serum and cerebrospinal fluid), SMPDB, LipidMAPS, YMDB, ECMDB, BMDB, FooDB, PlantCyc, ChEBI, T3DB, DrugBank, STOFF-IDENT, KNApSAcK, NANPDB, PubChem (biomolecules), and UNPD, were selected for structure searching. For FSEA, ions with more than $5\%$ relative abundance were defined as significant ions; non-significant ions were defined by undetected ontologies (default).
